A pitmaster favorite inspired by Meat Church. The complex blend of 23 flavors in Dr Pepper pairs naturally with smoky ribs, pulled pork, and grilled poultry. Simmered down with rich ketchup, molasses-forward brown sugar, and a generous punch of coarse butcher-grind black pepper, this sauce strikes the ideal balance between bold fruitiness and peppery warmth.

*Adapted from [Meat Church](https://www.meatchurch.com/blogs/recipes/dr-pepper-bbq-sauce).*

## Ingredients

- 1 can (12 oz) Dr Pepper (real sugar or Dublin-style Dr Pepper works best)
- 2 cups tomato ketchup (approx. 544g)
- 1 cup packed brown sugar (approx. 200g)
- 1 tablespoon coarse butcher-grind black pepper (approx. 7g)
- 1 teaspoon crushed red pepper flakes (adjust to heat preference)

## Instructions

1. **Whisk the Base:** Pour the Dr Pepper, ketchup, and brown sugar into a medium stainless steel or enameled saucepan. Add the coarse black pepper and crushed red pepper flakes. Whisk steadily until the sugar crystals dissolve into the liquid.
2. **Bring to a Boil:** Place the saucepan over medium-high heat and bring the mixture up to a lively boil, stirring regularly.
3. **Simmer to Glaze Consistency:** As soon as the sauce bubbles, lower the heat to medium-low to maintain a gentle simmer. Cook uncovered for 25 to 30 minutes, stirring every few minutes to ensure the sauce doesn't catch on the bottom of the pot. The sauce will darken in color, thicken, and reduce by roughly a third into a glossy glaze that clings to a spoon.
4. **Cool and Store:** Pull the pan from the heat and allow the barbecue sauce to cool to room temperature. Pour into clean glass mason jars or sauce bottles, cap tightly, and store in the refrigerator.

## Notes

- **Black Pepper Selection:** Use coarse butcher-grind black pepper rather than fine table pepper. The larger flecks provide authentic Texas barbecue texture and warm bite without turning bitter.
- **Grilling Application:** Because of the sugar content, apply this sauce during the final 10 to 15 minutes of grilling or smoking to avoid charring, or serve warm alongside sliced brisket, ribs, and pulled pork.
- **Shelf Life:** Keeps refrigerated in an airtight glass container for up to 3 to 4 weeks.
